# Power-of-Two Symmetry in Latin Squares

A Latin square is an n x n grid of n symbols with no symbol repeated in any row or column. We ask a symmetry question: read any two columns together, and for each ordered symbol pair (p, q) count the rows showing it. When (p, q) and (q, p) always appear equally often, the square is **pairwise reflection symmetric** (PRS). Remarkably, among group multiplication tables this happens exactly at the powers of two.

## The witnesses you can hold

The cleanest PRS squares are the XOR tables of the groups (Z/2)^k. Slide k and watch orders 2, 4, 8, 16, 32 appear:

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## Why these and no others?

The pivot is an algebraic property: a group's table is PRS **iff every element squares to the identity** (exponent two). Try it on cyclic groups, the Klein four-group, and elementary abelian groups; note that Z/4 has power-of-two order yet fails, because exponent two &mdash; not order alone &mdash; is the true condition:

{{interactive_demo:1}}

